### Question 2: Summarizing and Plotting

**Q2.1:** Bluebikes is Metro Boston's public bike share program. You are given a Bluebikes ridership dataset `q2_bike_trips.csv`. Each row represents a single trip, including information on start time, end time, start station, and end station. The dataset covers trips taken on September 3, 2025, the first day of our fall semester.

1.  Extract all trips starting at “MIT at Mass Ave / Amherst St” of this day. (1 points)

2.  the started_at_hour and ended_at_hour are extracted, the hour from the acutal time. Use started——at_hour, Create a graph that shows the number of trips by hour of the selected station of this day, with two lines representing member users and casual users. (6 points).

    Your maps may look similar (but not the same!) to the following example. Your theme and color choices can be different.

**Q2.2:** With waht you have, the all trips starting at “MIT at Mass Ave / Amherst St” of this day

1.  Create a table to show how many total trips ended at each station. In other words, count by “end_station_name”. (1 point)

2.  Filter out all stations with more than 5 trips. (1 point) You will use this result for Q3.

::: footnote
Data source: Bluebikes System Data. (2025). [Bluebikes Comprehensive Trip Histories](https://bluebikes.com/system-data).
:::

------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Question 3 Combining and Mapping

You are also provided a Bluebike station dataset `q3_bike_stations.xlsx`, which includes station names, ids, locations and total docks at each station.

-   Join your counts from Q2 (stations with more than 5 trips) with the station dataset. (2 points)
-   Use `sf` package, visualize these counts on a map using the stations' geographic locations to place points. (8 points).
    -   Use `q3_basemap.geojson` as your base map.
    -   Use point `size` to indicate the number of trips per station.
    -   Remove any stations without valid location information.

Your maps may look similar (but not the same1) to the following example. Your theme and color choice may also be different.

